title: Git & Version Control
page: version-control.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: Git is the engine and GitHub is one place to park it: learn the engine, then the workflow CSOH runs on it.

== Git is the engine; GitHub is one place to park it
layout: compare
section: top
col: Git | A fast, distributed version control system | Everyone has a full copy of the history
col: GitHub, GitLab or Bitbucket | A website that hosts git repositories | Adds pull requests, reviews, issues and automation
Version control is a database of every change ever made to a set of files, with the power to compare, undo, branch and merge that history.
notes:
Version control is a database of every change ever made to a set of files, and it lets you compare, undo, branch and merge that history.

Git is the tool that won: a fast, distributed version control system where everyone has a full copy of the history. GitHub, or GitLab, or Bitbucket, is a website that hosts git repositories and adds collaboration on top: pull requests, reviews, issues and automation.

People conflate git and GitHub, but they are different layers. Git is the engine, and GitHub is one place to park it. This deck teaches the engine first, then the workflow CSOH runs on it.

To follow along you need git installed and a free GitHub account. A terminal helps, but the page says you can do a surprising amount in the GitHub web UI alone.

== Version control remembers every state
layout: table
section: what-is-it
row: Superpower | What it gives you
row: History | What changed, when, by whom, and why if messages are good
row: Undo | Return any file, or the whole project, to any past state
row: Branching | Work on a change in isolation, then merge it back
row: Collaboration | Many people change one project; the VCS reconciles the work
A VCS records snapshots of your files over time, so you keep one set of files instead of report-final.doc and report-final-v2.doc.
notes:
A version control system, or VCS, records snapshots of your files over time. Instead of report-final.doc, report-final-v2.doc and a third "final" copy, you keep one set of files and the system remembers every state they have ever been in.

The page calls what that unlocks four superpowers. History: see exactly what changed, when, by whom, and, if the commit messages are good, why. Undo: return any file, or the whole project, to any past state. Branching: work on a change in isolation without disturbing the working version, then merge it back. Collaboration: many people change the same project at once, and the VCS reconciles the work.

== Centralized versus distributed
layout: compare
section: what-is-it
col: Centralized: CVS, Subversion | One server held the history | You checked files out from it
col: Distributed: git | A clone gets the entire history | Commit, branch, view history and diff offline | The network is needed only to share
That design, plus its speed, is why git is now the near-universal default, and why version control in practice usually means git.
notes:
Older systems such as CVS and Subversion were centralized: one server held the history, and you checked files out from it.

Git is distributed. When you clone a repository, you get the entire history on your machine, so you can commit, branch, view history and diff completely offline. You only need the network to share.

The page says that design, plus git's speed, is why git is now the near-universal default, and why version control in practice usually means git.

== The vocabulary you will see everywhere
layout: table
section: what-is-it
row: Term | Meaning
row: Repository (repo) | The project plus its entire history
row: Commit | One saved snapshot with author, timestamp, message and unique hash
row: Branch | A movable pointer to a line of commits, such as main
row: Remote | A copy hosted elsewhere, conventionally named origin
row: Clone / fork | Copy to your machine / copy to your account on the host
Branches are cheap to create, and the page calls them the heart of git's workflow.
notes:
A repository, or repo, is the project plus its entire history. A commit is one saved snapshot, with an author, a timestamp, a message and a unique hash like 27e293d7.

A branch is a movable pointer to a line of commits, for example main. Branches are cheap to create, and the page calls them the heart of git's workflow.

A remote is a copy of the repo hosted elsewhere, for example on GitHub, and it is conventionally named origin. To clone is to copy a repo to your machine; to fork is to copy it to your own account on the host.

== What version control gives you
layout: text
section: why-cool
- Nothing is lost: every committed state is recoverable
- git blame shows which commit last touched a line
- Branches make experiments free
- Code review, CI/CD and automated deploys all hang off git
- A signed, reviewed, immutable history is an audit trail
notes:
Nothing is ever lost. Every committed state is recoverable, so "I broke it" becomes a five-second revert, not a crisis.

The page says blame is a feature, not an accusation: git blame tells you which commit last touched a line, which is invaluable for "why is this here?" and for incident forensics.

Branches make experiments free. Try a risky change on a branch, and if it does not work out, delete the branch and it is gone; main never saw it.

Git is the substrate for everything else: code review, CI/CD, infrastructure as code and automated deploys all hang off it. The page says CSOH's GitHub Actions and Terraform only work because every change is a reviewable commit.

And it is a security control. A signed, reviewed, immutable history is an audit trail, and branch protection turns "who can change production?" into an enforceable policy.

== Where it bites
layout: list
section: why-cool
Ten minutes on the mental model saves ten hours of confusion.
item: The learning curve is real
item: Merge conflicts feel scary the first time
item: History can be rewritten, dangerously
item: Git is bad at big binaries
notes:
The learning curve is real. The mental model, on the next slide, is the part people skip, and the page says skipping it is why git feels like memorized incantations. Ten minutes on the model saves ten hours of confusion.

Merge conflicts feel scary the first time, but they are just git saying that two people changed the same lines and you decide. They are normal, and fixable.

History can be rewritten, dangerously. rebase, --force and reset --hard are powerful and can lose work or rewrite shared history, so respect them.

Git is bad at big binaries, because it is built for text. Large media bloats the repo forever. The page points to Git LFS, and notes that the "forever" cuts both ways, which is the secrets-in-history problem later in this deck.

== Four places your code lives
layout: figure
section: mental-model
figure: vc-fig-title
Almost every confusing git moment comes from not knowing which of four places a file is in.
notes:
The figure shows the four areas of git and the commands that move work between them.

git add moves work from the working directory to the staging area. git commit moves staging into the local repository. git push moves local history to the remote. git pull, or git clone, brings work from the remote back down to the working directory.

As the caption puts it, a commit is a snapshot, and a branch is just a sticky note pointing at one. The page says that once you learn this diagram, git stops being magic.

== The staging area: one idea per commit
layout: text
section: mental-model
Staging lets you commit some of your changes and not others, so each commit is one coherent idea. Edit everything, then assemble tidy commits: a normal and good workflow.
notes:
The staging area is the bit newcomers find odd and pros find indispensable.

It lets you commit some of your changes and not others, so each commit is one coherent idea. The page calls "edit everything, then assemble tidy commits" a normal and good workflow.

== Look around before you change anything
layout: code
section: commands
About a dozen commands get you a long way. Run git status constantly: it even suggests the command you probably want next.
```
# Start: copy a repo to your machine (you get the full history)
git clone https://github.com/CloudSecurityOfficeHours/csoh.org
cd csoh.org

# Look around - run status constantly; it tells you exactly where things stand
git status                 # what's changed, staged, and on which branch
git log --oneline -10      # recent history, one line each
git diff                   # what you've changed but not yet staged
```
notes:
The page says you can get a long way with about a dozen commands.

Start by cloning: you get the full history on your machine. Then look around. git status shows what has changed, what is staged, and which branch you are on. git log --oneline -10 shows recent history, one line each. git diff shows what you have changed but not yet staged.

The first habit that saves beginners the most pain is running git status obsessively. It tells you exactly where things stand, and it even suggests the command you probably want next.

== The core loop
layout: code
section: commands
Write commit messages that finish the sentence "If applied, this commit will...", imperative and specific.
```
# The core loop
git switch -c fix-typo     # create + move to a new branch (older: git checkout -b)
# ...edit files...
git add faq.html           # stage a specific file (git add -p to stage by chunk)
git commit -m "Fix typo in FAQ answer about CSPM"
git push -u origin fix-typo   # publish your branch to the remote

# Staying current
git switch main
git pull                   # fetch + merge the latest main from origin
```
notes:
This is the core loop. git switch -c creates a new branch and moves you onto it; the older form is git checkout -b.

Edit your files, then stage a specific file with git add, or stage by chunk with git add -p. Commit with a message, and push with -u origin to publish your branch to the remote.

To stay current, switch back to main and pull, which fetches and merges the latest main from origin.

The second habit is commit messages that finish the sentence "If applied, this commit will...", imperative and specific, like the real ones in the CSOH repository's history.

== A pull request is the unit of review
layout: text
section: branches-prs
A pull request (PR) asks for a branch to be merged back into main.
- Shows the exact diff
- Runs CI before anything merges
- Reviewers comment on specific lines
- Approved and green, it merges to main
notes:
A branch is an isolated line of work. You branch off main, make commits, and when the change is ready you ask for it to be merged back. On a host like GitHub that request is a pull request, the unit of collaboration and review.

A PR shows the exact diff, every added and removed line, so a reviewer sees precisely what would change. It runs automated checks before anything merges: linting, tests, and on CSOH the HTML validator and link checker. It is a conversation: reviewers comment on specific lines, you push fixes, and the PR updates.

When it is approved and green, it merges to main, and on the CSOH repo that triggers the deploy.

== Keep each PR to one idea
layout: text
section: branches-prs
The everyday flow: branch, commit, push, open PR, review, merge, delete branch. Small, focused PRs get reviewed fast; giant ones sit for days.
notes:
The everyday flow is branch, commit, push, open a PR, review, merge, and delete the branch.

The page's advice is to keep each PR to one idea, because small, focused PRs get reviewed fast and giant ones sit for days.

== Contributing to CSOH: fork, branch, pull request
layout: checklist
section: tour
You don't need write access to help. For a one-line fix, GitHub's pencil icon edits in the browser and offers to open the PR.
check: Fork the repo on GitHub, which creates your own copy
check: Clone your fork
check: Branch for your change
check: Edit, stage and commit
check: Push to your fork and open a pull request
notes:
This whole website lives in one public repository, and the page says reading it is a fine way to learn: real commits, real PRs, real history.

You do not need write access to help. The standard open-source flow, documented in the CONTRIBUTING.md guide, is: fork the repo on GitHub, which creates your own copy; clone your fork; branch for your change; edit, stage and commit; then push to your fork and open a pull request on GitHub.

For a one-line fix you do not even need a terminal. GitHub's pencil icon lets you edit a file in the browser, and "Commit changes" offers to open the PR for you. Either way, a maintainer reviews it.

== What fires when something lands on main
layout: compare
section: tour
col: Housekeeping: site-update-deploy.yml | Regenerates SRI hashes, checks URL safety, normalizes URLs | Builds preview images and the sitemap | Commits the results back to main
col: Deploy: deploy.yml | Builds the site once | Publishes active/active to AWS, GCP and Azure behind Cloudflare | Authenticates to each with keyless OIDC
Maintainers can commit routine, low-risk edits straight to main. A merge or push to main is the deploy trigger.
notes:
Maintainers with write access can commit to main directly for routine, low-risk edits, which immediately triggers the build-and-deploy pipeline. The page says that speed is exactly why the safety net matters: the protections and automated checks are what make a direct push to production safe to do.

A merge or push to main is the deploy trigger, and two GitHub Actions workflows fire automatically. Housekeeping, site-update-deploy.yml, regenerates Subresource Integrity hashes, checks URL safety, normalizes URLs, builds preview images and the sitemap, and commits the results back to main. Deploy, deploy.yml, builds the site once and publishes it active/active to three clouds, AWS, GCP and Azure, behind Cloudflare, authenticating to each with keyless OIDC.

So a two-character typo fix in a PR becomes a fully-tested, multi-cloud production deploy with no manual steps. The page says that pipeline is the entire reason CSOH keeps changes small and reviewable, and that git is what makes shipping 20 times a day, safely, possible.

== The PR checklist CSOH uses
layout: checklist
section: tour
Green checks are a merge requirement.
check: Clear and focused: one idea per PR
check: Spelling and links check out
check: Looks right in light and dark mode
check: A descriptive commit message
check: CI green: the lint workflow and W3C HTML validator pass
notes:
Before a change merges, it should clear the bar in the contributor guide.

The change is clear and focused, one idea per PR. Spelling and links check out. It looks right in both light and dark mode. The commit message is descriptive. And CI is green, meaning the lint workflow, which runs actionlint, ruff and yamllint, and the W3C HTML validator both pass.

The page is firm that green checks are a merge requirement, not a suggestion.

== Conflicts are normal; detached HEAD isn't broken
layout: table
section: concepts
row: Moment | What is happening | What to do
row: Merge conflict | Two branches changed the same lines | Edit to the version you want, delete markers, git add, continue
row: Detached HEAD | You checked out a commit, not a branch | To keep changes, git switch -c new-branch before moving away
No data is lost in a conflict, and small frequent merges keep conflicts tiny.
notes:
When two branches change the same lines, git cannot guess who is right, so it marks the spot with conflict markers, rows of less-than signs, equals signs and greater-than signs, and asks you to choose. Edit the file to the version you want, delete the markers, git add it, and continue. That is the whole ritual. No data is lost, and small frequent merges keep conflicts tiny.

Checking out a specific commit instead of a branch puts you in detached HEAD: you are looking at history, not on a branch. Look around all you like. If you want to keep changes made there, create a branch with git switch -c new-branch before moving away.

== merge versus rebase
layout: compare
section: concepts
col: git merge | Joins two branches and records a merge commit | True history, occasionally messy
col: git rebase | Replays your commits on top of the latest base | Linear, tidy history | Rewrites your commits, with new hashes
Rule of thumb: rebase your own un-pushed work to tidy it; don't rebase commits other people have already pulled.
notes:
git merge joins two branches and records a merge commit: true history, occasionally messy. git rebase replays your commits on top of the latest base for a linear, tidy history, but it rewrites your commits, giving them new hashes.

The rule of thumb: rebase your own un-pushed work to tidy it, and do not rebase commits other people have already pulled.

A related habit: if main moved while you were working, git pull --rebase replays your local commits on top of the new main instead of creating a merge bubble. CSOH's CI uses exactly this pattern when a workflow needs to commit back to a branch that may have moved: git push, and if that fails, git pull --rebase and push again.

== Undo: revert is safe, reset is sharp
layout: compare
section: concepts
col: git revert | Makes a new commit that undoes an old one | Safe on shared branches: no history rewrite
col: git reset | Moves the branch pointer backward | Can discard commits | --hard also throws away working changes
Reach for revert on anything already pushed; keep reset --hard for local-only cleanup you're sure about.
notes:
git revert, given a commit, makes a new commit that undoes the old one. It is safe on shared branches because it does not rewrite history.

git reset moves the branch pointer backward and can discard commits, and --hard also throws away working changes.

The page's advice: reach for revert on anything already pushed, and keep reset --hard for local-only cleanup you are sure about.

== .gitignore: keep junk and secrets out
layout: text
section: concepts
A .gitignore lists paths git should never track: build output, dependency folders, local config and, critically, anything secret. Set it up before your first commit.
notes:
A .gitignore file lists paths git should never track: build output, dependency folders, local config, and, critically, anything secret. The CSOH repo ignores things like .env, the Python .venv folder, and Terraform's .terraform folder and state.

Set this up before your first commit, because un-committing something later is harder than never committing it.

== Committed a secret? The history remembers
layout: text
section: concepts
Deleting a secret in a new commit does not remove it: it is still in the previous commit, for anyone who clones. Assume it is compromised, rotate it immediately, then scrub history.
notes:
The page calls this the one that catches people. Deleting a secret in a new commit does not remove it from history. It is still in the previous commit, forever, for anyone who clones the repo.

If you commit a key, assume it is compromised. Rotate it, which means invalidate it, immediately, then scrub history with BFG or git filter-repo. Prevention beats cleanup.

The rule CSOH drills into every contributor: git history is permanent by design. That is a superpower for auditing and a trap for secrets, because a key committed once is a key leaked forever. Rotate, do not just delete.

== The security baseline
layout: checklist
section: security
Version control is a security boundary: it decides what reaches production and leaves an audit trail of who changed what.
check: Never commit secrets; if one slips in, rotate it
check: Turn on secret scanning and push protection; add a pre-commit hook
check: Protect main with branch protection rules
check: Sign your commits
check: Turn on 2FA and use least-privilege tokens
check: Treat third-party PRs as untrusted input
check: Pin Actions to commit SHAs; give workflows least privilege
notes:
As a cloud-security community, the page says this is the part CSOH cares most about.

Never commit secrets: no API keys, tokens, passwords or private keys in the repo. Use environment variables or a secrets manager, and a .gitignore that covers .env and friends. If one slips in, rotate it, because history is forever.

Scan for secrets automatically. Turn on GitHub secret scanning and push protection, and add a pre-commit hook with gitleaks or TruffleHog, so a key is caught before it is ever pushed.

Pin and review your CI. The automation that runs on every push is itself attackable, so pin Actions to commit SHAs and give workflows least-privilege permissions. The GitHub Actions page covers this in depth.

The next slide takes branch protection, signing and tokens, and the last question comes back to third-party PRs.

== Protect main, sign commits, scope tokens
layout: table
section: security
row: Control | What it does
row: Branch protection | Require a PR, review and green checks; block force-pushes
row: Signed commits | GPG or SSH signatures prove a commit came from you
row: 2FA | Hardware or app-based, on your GitHub account
row: Fine-grained tokens | Prefer short-lived, one-repo tokens to classic all-powerful PATs
Branch protection makes "don't break production" something the platform enforces.
notes:
Branch protection rules, such as requiring a PR, requiring review, requiring green status checks and blocking force-pushes, turn "don't break production" into something the platform enforces, not something you hope for.

GPG- or SSH-signed commits earn a "Verified" badge and prove a commit really came from you, defeating spoofed author names. For a high-assurance history, require signed commits on protected branches.

Turn on hardware or app-based 2FA on your GitHub account, and prefer fine-grained, short-lived personal access tokens scoped to one repo over classic all-powerful PATs.

== What can you still do offline?
layout: question
section: what-is-it
You cloned a repository, and then the network went away.
notes:
This checks the difference between git and the host it is parked on. Ask it and let the room answer before moving on.

== Everything except share
layout: answer
section: what-is-it
A clone holds the entire history, so you can commit, branch, view history and diff completely offline. You only need the network to share.
notes:
Git is distributed. When you clone a repository you get the entire history on your machine, so committing, branching, viewing history and diffing all work completely offline. You only need the network to share.

That is the difference from centralized systems such as CVS and Subversion, where one server held the history.

== How far do you trust a pull request from a fork?
layout: question
section: security
A contributor without write access forked the repository and opened a pull request.
notes:
This is the third-party PR item from the security baseline. Ask it before showing the answer.

== As untrusted input
layout: answer
section: security
Review forked PRs carefully and never let them run with your secrets. The pull_request_target footgun has leaked credentials on major projects.
notes:
The page says to treat third-party PRs as untrusted input. Review forked PRs carefully, and never let them run with your secrets. The pull_request_target footgun has leaked credentials on major projects.

The page points to its breach kill chains and to the GitHub Actions page's security section for more on how that goes wrong.
